  {
    "$schema": "https://ncpif.org/ncsb.schema.json",
    "id": "black-rail-habitat-restoration-pilot-project-and-internship-at-alligator-river-nwr",
    "title": "Black Rail Habitat Restoration Pilot Project and Internship at Alligator River NWR",
    "itemType": "project",
    "description": "The eastern black rail is a sparrow sized secretive marsh bird that nests on or near the ground in dense vegetative cover over water or moist soil (USFWS 2018). They inhabit very shallowly flooded, densely vegetated salt, brackish, and freshwater marshes. They appear to tolerate a very narrow range of conditions within these habitats with the unifying features being persistent water coverage and extremely dense herbaceous vegetation. Too much or too little water at any time during the breeding season results in abandonment of the site (USFWS 2018). Declining populations across the Atlantic Coast range led to the USFWS listing the eastern black rail as threatened under the Endangered Species Act in 2020. Callback surveys conducted in 2017 and 2018 did detect black rails at Alligator River NWR, but we do not have current population estimates or understand associations with different habitat types. Discussions between USFWS and partners at working group meetings proposed additional efforts occur on the Refuge to evaluate the presence of black rails. One suggestion was to convert existing impoundments managed historically for wintering waterfowl through cooperative farming or maintenance of moist soil units. Efforts began in fall of 2021 to modify the Creef A1N impoundment. Additional maintenance actions including rehabilitation of the dikes, disking/grading, and the installation of a new water control structure under Peterson Road are scheduled for spring 2022 (late March-June). After these actions are completed, water will be put back in A1N immediately to initiate this experiment. Manipulation of water levels within this unit will be a proof-of-concept experiment to see if we can maintain shallow water areas year-round as semi-aquatic black rail habitat. Results will then inform the feasibility of converting other habitats across the Refuge complex for these declining protected species. Project objectives include: To convert existing impoundments managed historically for wintering waterfowl through cooperative farming or maintenance of moist soil units to shallow water impoundments for rails.",
